#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198023 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198381 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65a25c72-9fa7-58fc-bf45-9cc2761696ea" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Division Problems as Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "41696b59-d81e-5c49-a967-141f67934aa1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12359" :question/problem "Find a fraction that's equivalent to $1 \\div (-7)$." :question/worked-solution "We have to divide a positive number $({\\color{blue}{1}})$ by a negative one $({\\color{red}{-7}})$. So the result will be negative:\n\n$$\nA\n$$\n\nTo write down the number that goes in the box, recall that\n\n$$\n1 \\div 7 = \\dfrac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}} \\div {\\color{red}{(-7)}} = {\\color{red}-\\dfrac{1}{7}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198024 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fe851f3-482c-5426-87b2-32ec3c1b40f6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Division Problems as Mixed Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "e40e5f9e-aab7-53ed-8850-bd855f2b97a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12360" :question/problem "Find a fraction that's equivalent to $(-5) \\div (-3)$." :question/worked-solution "Here, we are dividing a negative number $({\\color{red}-5})$ by another negative number $({\\color{red}-3})$. So the result will be positive:\n\n$$\nA\n$$\n\nNow, to write down the fraction that goes in the box, recall that\n\n$$\n5 \\div 3 = 1 \\,\\textrm{R}\\, 2 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-5)} \\div {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{blue} 1 \\, \\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198025 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d17ce34f-7d65-5f44-a1c0-dfd3c2ea55f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalent Fractions With Negative Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "a650b9e6-c97c-5471-af2d-76a5cfb0caff" :question/math-academy-id "e-12361" :question/problem "What is the missing part of the expression below?\n$\\dfrac{3}{14} = \\dfrac{-3}{\\bbox[1pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}$" :question/worked-solution "A positive number can be obtained if we divide two numbers that are either both positive or both negative.\n\nSo, given a positive fraction, we have the following equivalent notations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{14} = \\dfrac{-3}{-14}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we see that the numerator on the right-hand side is negative $({{\\color{red}-3}})$. Therefore, the missing denominator must be $({{\\color{red}-14}}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{14} = \\dfrac{-3}{\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\color{red}-14}}\n$$"}]}
